WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.037
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.337 --> 00:00:08.642
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:17.121
Unit three: Comparative Politics. Today you will compare political systems precisely with comparative clauses and auxiliaries.

4
00:00:17.421 --> 00:00:19.922
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:24.297
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:26.523
Political system.

7
00:00:27.517 --> 00:00:31.478
Political system means the institutions through which a society is governed.

8
00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:35.569
Each political system balances freedom and order differently.

9
00:00:36.183 --> 00:00:39.242
Your turn. Say: Political system.

10
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.438
Political system.

11
00:00:44.183 --> 00:00:45.196
Federalism.

12
00:00:46.183 --> 00:00:51.318
Federalism means a system that divides power between central and regional governments.

13
00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:54.799
Federalism gives states control over education.

14
00:00:55.383 --> 00:00:58.082
Your turn. Say: Federalism.

15
00:01:00.933 --> 00:01:01.981
Federalism.

16
00:01:02.717 --> 00:01:03.880
Parliamentary.

17
00:01:04.883 --> 00:01:09.135
Parliamentary means describing a system where the government is drawn from parliament.

18
00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:13.619
In a parliamentary system, the prime minister must keep a majority.

19
00:01:14.217 --> 00:01:16.544
Your turn. Say: Parliamentary.

20
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:20.552
Parliamentary.

21
00:01:21.317 --> 00:01:22.608
Authoritarian.

22
00:01:23.617 --> 00:01:27.787
Authoritarian means demanding strict obedience and limiting freedoms.

23
00:01:28.383 --> 00:01:31.439
Authoritarian regimes often control the media.

24
00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.900
Your turn. Say: Authoritarian.

25
00:01:37.733 --> 00:01:39.060
Authoritarian.

26
00:01:39.817 --> 00:01:41.410
Checks and balances.

27
00:01:42.417 --> 00:01:46.738
Checks and balances means rules that stop any branch of power from dominating.

28
00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:50.452
Courts provide checks and balances on the executive.

29
00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:54.782
Your turn. Say: Checks and balances.

30
00:01:57.633 --> 00:01:59.319
Checks and balances.

31
00:02:00.083 --> 00:02:01.363
Electoral system.

32
00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:05.627
Electoral system means the rules that turn votes into seats.

33
00:02:06.217 --> 00:02:09.354
Proportional electoral systems favour coalitions.

34
00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:13.102
Your turn. Say: Electoral system.

35
00:02:15.967 --> 00:02:17.421
Electoral system.

36
00:02:18.183 --> 00:02:19.428
Governance model.

37
00:02:20.417 --> 00:02:24.053
Governance model means an overall design for how decisions are made.

38
00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:27.811
The city adopted a participatory governance model.

39
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:31.034
Your turn. Say: Governance model.

40
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.075
Governance model.

41
00:02:35.817 --> 00:02:37.305
Political culture.

42
00:02:38.317 --> 00:02:42.360
Political culture means shared attitudes and beliefs about politics.

43
00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.192
A strong political culture of debate supports democracy.

44
00:02:46.783 --> 00:02:49.923
Your turn. Say: Political culture.

45
00:02:52.767 --> 00:02:54.081
Political culture.

46
00:02:54.817 --> 00:02:55.806
Coalition.

47
00:02:56.817 --> 00:03:00.175
Coalition means an alliance of parties formed to govern together.

48
00:03:00.783 --> 00:03:03.270
The coalition collapsed after six months.

49
00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:06.640
Your turn. Say: Coalition.

50
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:10.466
Coalition.

51
00:03:11.217 --> 00:03:12.171
Mandate.

52
00:03:13.183 --> 00:03:16.518
Mandate means the authority given to a government by voters.

53
00:03:17.117 --> 00:03:19.453
The party won a clear mandate for reform.

54
00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.575
Your turn. Say: Mandate.

55
00:03:25.433 --> 00:03:26.307
Mandate.

56
00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.925
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:31.783 --> 00:03:37.919
Visal, in your presentation you compared federal and centralized states. What was your main finding?

58
00:03:39.017 --> 00:03:47.854
That federalism distributes power far more widely than a centralized system does, so regions can experiment with their own policies.

59
00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:51.402
Doesn't that make national decisions slower?

60
00:03:52.517 --> 00:04:01.647
It does. A unitary state can act faster than a federal one can, but it is also less responsive to local needs.

61
00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.493
And where do coalitions fit into your comparison?

62
00:04:06.583 --> 00:04:15.157
Proportional systems produce coalitions more often than majoritarian ones do. They are less decisive, though arguably more representative.

63
00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.183
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.862
Grammar focus: Comparative clauses.

65
00:04:22.162 --> 00:04:23.291
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:23.983 --> 00:04:28.131
Federalism distributes power differently than a centralized system does.

67
00:04:29.017 --> 00:04:32.421
A unitary state can act faster than a federal one can.

68
00:04:33.317 --> 00:04:37.418
Coalitions are far less stable than single-party governments tend to be.

69
00:04:38.367 --> 00:04:48.403
In formal comparisons, repeat the auxiliary instead of the full verb: '…than a centralized system does'. Modify comparatives with far, considerably or slightly for precision.

70
00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:54.275
Be careful! Don't say: A unitary state acts faster than a federal one is.

71
00:04:56.233 --> 00:05:00.474
Say: A unitary state acts faster than a federal one does.

72
00:05:00.774 --> 00:05:04.631
Repeat the correct auxiliary: acts → does.

73
00:05:05.433 --> 00:05:07.993
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.184
No Perfect System.

75
00:05:10.484 --> 00:05:17.000
Political scientists rarely ask which system is best; they ask what each system does well and at what cost.

76
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:25.131
Parliamentary systems tend to produce governments more quickly than presidential systems do, since the executive depends on a majority.

77
00:05:26.133 --> 00:05:32.580
Federal states, by contrast, protect regional diversity far more effectively than unitary states can.

78
00:05:33.567 --> 00:05:38.805
However, every design relies on a political culture that values compromise and the rule of law.

79
00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:44.644
Without such a culture, even the most elegant checks and balances can be quietly ignored.

80
00:05:45.733 --> 00:05:49.921
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:50.783 --> 00:05:54.385
A unitary state can respond faster than a federal one blank.

82
00:05:54.685 --> 00:05:57.700
A, does. B, can. C, is.

83
00:06:01.233 --> 00:06:02.896
The answer is B: can.

84
00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.166
Choose the most precise comparison.

85
00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:14.043
A, Coalitions are considerably less stable.. B, Coalitions are very more unstable.. C, Coalitions are much unstable..

86
00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:21.306
The answer is A: Coalitions are considerably less stable..

87
00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:25.065
The authority voters give a government is its blank.

88
00:06:25.365 --> 00:06:29.355
A, coalition. B, mandate. C, federalism.

89
00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:35.097
The answer is B: mandate.

90
00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.145
Which collocation is correct?

91
00:06:38.445 --> 00:06:43.050
A, make a coalition. B, form a coalition. C, do a coalition.

92
00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:49.076
The answer is B: form a coalition.

93
00:06:50.217 --> 00:06:53.586
According to the reading, what do all systems depend on?

94
00:06:53.886 --> 00:06:59.954
A, a strong president. B, a large parliament. C, a political culture of compromise.

95
00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:07.241
The answer is C: a political culture of compromise.

96
00:07:08.433 --> 00:07:11.422
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:12.283 --> 00:07:15.397
What political system do you think works best, and why?

98
00:07:32.183 --> 00:07:33.672
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:33.972 --> 00:08:09.303
In my view, a parliamentary democracy with strong checks and balances works best for most countries. It allows a government to act more quickly than a divided presidential system usually can, while still being accountable to parliament. Germany, for example, combines federalism with coalition government and remains remarkably stable. On the other hand, no system works without a political culture of compromise. So the institutions matter, but the habits of citizens matter just as much.

100
00:08:09.603 --> 00:08:11.254
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:16.451
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and comparative clauses.

102
00:08:16.751 --> 00:08:22.062
Today's value is civic responsibility. Take care of your city, because it is everyone's home.

103
00:08:22.362 --> 00:08:25.014
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

104
00:08:25.314 --> 00:08:28.814
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

